{% comment %}
  Renders a wave-shaped SVG divider, used to curve into a section below it,
  optionally with a line of text set along the inside of the curve.

  Accepts:
    fill     - CSS colour for the wave (defaults to the dark brown scheme-5 tone)
    text     - optional label repeated along the inside of the curve, e.g. "Reviews ·"
    repeat   - how many times to repeat it (default 16, enough to overrun 1440 wide)
    text_color - CSS colour for that text (defaults to the brand terracotta)

  The curve is set with preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMin slice" rather than "none".
  "none" stretches the viewBox to whatever box CSS gives it, independently on each
  axis - fine for a plain shape, but it would squash the text badly at narrow
  widths (at 430px the horizontal scale is about half the vertical). "slice"
  scales both axes by the same factor and crops the overflow instead, so the
  lettering keeps its shape at every width. Anchored to the top (yMin) so the
  crop always comes off the bottom, which is solid fill and runs into the section
  below - cropping the top would clip the peak of the curve flat.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign wave_text = text | default: ''
  assign wave_repeat = repeat | default: 16
  assign wave_class = 'wave-divider'
  if name != blank
    assign wave_class = wave_class | append: ' wave-divider--' | append: name
  endif
  if flip
    assign wave_class = wave_class | append: ' wave-divider--flipped'
  endif
-%}

<div class="{{ wave_class }}" style="--wave-fill: {{ fill | default: '#754B2A' }};">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 1440 100"
    preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMin slice"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
    {% if wave_text == blank %}aria-hidden="true"{% else %}role="img" aria-label="{{ wave_text | strip_html | strip }}"{% endif %}
  >
    <path
      d="M0,40 C240,100 480,0 720,40 C960,80 1200,20 1440,50 L1440,100 L0,100 Z"
      fill="var(--wave-fill)"
    ></path>

    {%- if wave_text != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The same curve shifted down, so the text rides parallel to the edge of
        the wave rather than sitting on it. Kept as its own path (not the fill
        path above) because that one closes along the bottom of the box, which
        the text would then run back along.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <defs>
        <path id="wave-divider-textpath" d="M0,74 C240,134 480,34 720,74 C960,114 1200,54 1440,84" fill="none"></path>
      </defs>
      <text class="wave-divider__text" fill="{{ text_color | default: '#cd643f' }}">
        <textPath href="#wave-divider-textpath" xlink:href="#wave-divider-textpath">
          {%- for i in (1..wave_repeat) -%}{{ wave_text }}&#160;&#160;{%- endfor -%}
        </textPath>
      </text>
    {%- endif -%}
  </svg>
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  .wave-divider {
    position: relative;
    /* Above the section below it: a backdrop in that section may reach up
       behind the wave so its texture starts at the curve rather than at the
       section boundary, and the wave has to stay the thing you see. */
    z-index: 1;
    line-height: 0;
    margin-block-end: -1px;
  }

  /* Full-bleed the wave.

     Section wrappers carry a left gutter so content clears the vertical script
     rail, and the block inside the section is trimmed in again. That left the
     wave starting ~56px from the left edge and stopping ~40px short of the
     right, while the coloured section it curves into is edge to edge - so both
     ends of the curve met the flat top of the band below in a visible step.
     The wave is decoration, not content, so it ignores the rail gutter and
     spans the viewport like the band it introduces.

     The #MainContent prefix is load-bearing: the gutter comes from
     "#MainContent > div.shopify-section" in layout/theme.liquid, and an id
     beats a plain class, so a .shopify-section:has(...) rule alone loses to it
     and the wave stays inset. */
  #MainContent > div.shopify-section:has(.wave-divider) {
    padding-inline: 0;
  }

  #MainContent > div.shopify-section:has(.wave-divider) .section-content-wrapper > * {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .wave-divider svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 72px;
  }

  /* Closing the band: the same curve mirrored, so a coloured section is
     bookended rather than entering on a curve and leaving on a hard edge.
     Flipped ones carry no text - it would read upside down. */
  .wave-divider--flipped {
    margin-block-end: 0;
    margin-block-start: -1px;
  }

  .wave-divider--flipped svg {
    transform: scaleY(-1);
  }

  .wave-divider__text {
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 26px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .wave-divider svg {
      height: 124px;
    }

    .wave-divider__text {
      font-size: 22px;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
